{"type":"message","id":"ae83d442","parentId":"76df8105","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:37.705Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"That register file doesn't control the heartbeat interval. The probe function I already looked at (probe-Dd4yPYHJ.js) has:\n- PROBE_SUCCESS_TTL_MS = 600 * 1e3 = 10 minutes\n- PROBE_ERROR_TTL_MS = 60 * 1e3 = 1 minute\n\nAnd the probe is called by `probeFeishu()`. The issue is that the actual problem is at the Lark API level - the `/open-apis/bot/v1/openclaw_bot/ping` endpoint is returning HTTP 429.\n\nThe key insight from the code comment:\n```\n* Gateway health checks call probeFeishu() every minute; without caching this\n* burns ~43,200 calls/month, easily exceeding Feishu's free-tier quota.\n```\n\nSo the gateway health checks call probe every minute, but the probe has a cache with 10 minute TTL for success and 1 minute TTL for errors. But despite the caching, the ping itself is failing with 429.\n\nThe actual issue is that the custom bot ping endpoint `openclaw_bot/ping` is getting rate-limited by Lark. This might be at the Lark application level or tenant level rate limiting.\n\nLet me check if this ping endpoint is a standard Lark bot API or a custom one. The endpoint `/open-apis/bot/v1/openclaw_bot/ping` looks like a custom method (with `openclaw_bot` in the path), so it might not be a standard Lark API at all. It's calling the Lark bot's custom method endpoint.\n\nLooking at the Lark SDK documentation, the bot's custom methods like ping would be at `/open-apis/bot/v1/openclaw_bot/ping`. But the issue is that Lark has rate limits on these APIs.\n\nThe real fix options are:\n1. The probe caching already has error TTL of 1 minute, so it doesn't hammer the API during failures\n2. But the gateway health check is calling probe every minute, and the error cache only lasts 1 minute, so it keeps hitting the endpoint\n\nActually wait - looking more carefully at the error logs, this isn't just the gateway 